Yes, a video of someone preparing to commit an offence under the Law and Order Disruption Crimes (Speedy Trial) Act, 2002 can be used as evidence.

Section 14 of the Act provides that if any member of the police or law enforcement agency, or any other person, records or captures a film or still image of any event relating to the commission, preparation for commission, or assistance in the commission of an offence under this Act — including any conversation or discussion recorded on tape or disk — such film, still image, tape, or disk shall be admissible as evidence in the trial of such offence (Law and Order Disruption Crimes (Speedy Trial) Act, 2002, Section 14).

The term “offence” under this Act includes “law and order disruption offences,” which are defined in Section 2 to encompass intentional acts such as:  
- demanding or collecting money or property by threat or unlawful force;  
- obstructing movement on roads, railways, waterways, or airways;  
- intentionally damaging vehicles;  
- destroying state or private property;  
- snatching or attempting to snatch property; or  
- creating fear, terror, or disorder through organized displays of strength in public places (Law and Order Disruption Crimes (Speedy Trial) Act, 2002, Section 2).

Preparation for such an offence — including surveillance, gathering tools, coordination, or other preparatory acts captured on video — falls within the scope of “events relating to... preparation for commission” as explicitly covered by Section 14.

Therefore, a video documenting such preparatory conduct is admissible as evidence in proceedings under this Act.

